<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco I Gattilusio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1384">1384</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco I <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gattilusio">Gattilusio</a> was a Genoese adventurer who helped the Byzantine emperor John V Palaiologos regain his throne from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a> in 1354. In reward he received the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and the hand of John V's sister Maria, founding the Gattilusio lordship of Lesbos. He ruled from Mytilene until he was killed in an earthquake in 1384.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-i-gattilusio.jpg" alt="Francesco I Gattilusio" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown 14th century mint master · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denaro_of_Francesco_I_Gattilusio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gattilusio">Gattilusio family</a> came from the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. The parents of Francesco and his brother Niccolò are not known, though Anthony Luttrell argues from the heraldry of their inscriptions that their mother was a Doria. Francesco enters the historical record as a freebooter or pirate. In 1354 he and his crew landed on Tenedos, where John V Palaiologos was living, in conflict with his co-emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a> and Matthew Kantakouzenos. The two men agreed that Francesco would help John regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in return for the hand of John's sister Irene, renamed Maria Palaiologina.</p><p>In early December 1354 Francesco brought a fleet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> by night, claiming that a ship was sinking and promising the sentries a share of its cargo for their help. When they opened a gate, he led about five hundred armed men through it, took command of the walls and roused the citizens with shouts for John V. Riots followed, and by 4 December John VI had resigned and retired to a monastery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>John V kept the agreement, and on 17 July 1355 Francesco was made Archon, or Lord, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, which the emperor had offered as his sister's dowry. He ruled it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Byzantine emperor. In 1366 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-vi-count-of-savoy">Amadeus VI, Count of Savoy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> in a campaign for the empire; they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> from the Ottoman Murad I after two days of fighting, and in October the fleets of Amadeus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gattilusio">Gattilusio</a> captured Sozopol, Mesembria, Emona, Anchialo and other places on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. In 1369 he went with John V and Demetrios Kydones to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where the emperor accepted the Catholic faith on 18 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 6 August 1384 an earthquake struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and killed Francesco and his two eldest sons, Andronico and Domenico. Kydones wrote soon after from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> of how the two sons' intended brides arrived from afar and searched the ruins for the bodies of husbands they had never seen. The third son, Jacopo, survived and ruled Lesbos as Francesco II, continuing the line that Francesco I had founded as the first of his family to rule the island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francesco I Gattilusio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ainos (Enez), an old Greek port at the mouth of the Hebrus (Maritsa), came under the control of Niccolo Gattilusio, brother of <b>Francesco I Gattilusio</b> of the Lordship of Lesbos, around 1384.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ainos">Lordship of Ainos</a></li><li><blockquote>The Genoese adventurer <b>Francesco Gattilusio</b> helped the Byzantine emperor John V Palaiologos regain his throne in 1354.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-lesbos">Lordship of Lesbos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1374" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francesco I Gattilusio"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5544 3760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5544 3760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1374">What was here in 1374? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
